Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  without a doubt the most depicted figure  in art is jesus paintings stained glass  mosaics i'm sure you've seen them all  before but what about this one a  disturbing image of jesus as a  three-headed hybrid a strange  multi-headed being with one body three  noses and four eyes these images are so  weird in fact that they were even banned  by the catholic church  perhaps because they stem from ancient  pagan mythologies or that they appear in  portraits of the devil welcome to  strange christian art  this episode  three-headed jesus  how did this unusual depiction of jesus  come about and what does it even mean  well it's all to do with illustrating a  core christian idea about god the  trinity it's the idea that god exists as  three distinct persons the father the  son and the holy spirit christians  believe that all of them are god yet  simultaneously the father is not the son  the son is not the spirit and the spirit  is not the father confused yes that's  normal it's a confusing idea that defies  logic but maybe that's the point and for  centuries people have argued over what  it does and does not mean and if you're  an artist tasked with representing all  this you're faced with a bit of a  problem how do you make this diagram  into a nice painting some artists have  tried to depict the trinity as three men  of the same appearance but then you risk  implying that you're worshipping three  gods an idea known as tritheism and if  you paint just one of the divine persons  then you could be accused of ignoring  the other two to get around these  problems artists found an ingenious  loophole to combine them all together as  a result you get this an amalgamation of  the three divine persons into one being  whilst it's an orthodox design it's  pretty orthodox religiously speaking it  roughly manages to capture the idea that  god is three divine persons in one oh  and to be clear this is not what god is  supposed to look like it's more of a  symbolic representation  at least i hope this method of combining  figures together has led to some pretty  fascinating artworks take a look at the  6th century icon of jesus incidentally  it's one of the oldest depictions of him  around now if you look closely you'll  notice that each side of the face looks  ever so slightly different one half  looks quite stern and the other is more  gentle if i mirror the sides for you  you'll get a better idea of what i mean  it's believed that this half represents  his divine nature with him pointing up  to heaven the other half is his human  nature with his gospel book for the  people on earth despite these very  clever paintings by imaginative artists  depicting jesus or god with 100  theological accuracy is a bit of a  tricky task the fact is these religious  doctrines don't translate well onto  canvas as doing so often results in  confusion this has led many to simply  give up trying or even ban figurative  depictions of god entirely  [Music]  returning now to our beautifully strange  three-headed jesus this kind of image  has its roots in a number of ancient  mythologies take the roman god janus as  an example he's the patron deity of time  new beginnings and change to symbolize  this he has one face looking forwards  and the other looking backwards you may  be aware that the month of january is  named after him a time when one is  looking at the year ahead and the year  just passed going further back and we  have the celtic god lugus  in irish mythology he is a warrior deity  and is associated with law and kingship  this stone carving from ancient france  dates back to 200 bce despite looking  like jesus it predates christianity by a  few centuries there are plenty of these  three-headed deities to be found from  the slavic god triglav whose heads  symbolize the sky earth and the  underworld to hecate the greek goddess  of sorcery and magic  of course there are many outside of  europe too particularly in hindu  traditions with the god brahma the  three-headed divinity of creation  three-headed deities were surprisingly  commonplace and it's believed that when  christianity started to spread in europe  it began absorbing the various artistic  motifs that were used to depict these  pre-existing gods they provided the  necessary inspiration for early  christian artists who were having a  tough time depicting the trinity  [Music]  by the middle ages and early renaissance  our three-headed jesus was a  well-established art form in fact it's  everywhere appearing in the unlikeliest  of places in some manuscripts he has a  branching neck that sprouts three  hydra-like heads in others he has one  head but the faces are fused together in  some cases it's just a floating  disembodied head  they may seem strange to us today but in  medieval europe it was widely accepted  perhaps it's because there was a  fascination with all things  unexplainable and otherworldly in the  medieval imagination there seemed to be  a connection between the distorted and  the divine  although three heads were a popular way  to imagine god it also became a way of  imagining his counterpart the devil in  dante's inferno an epic description of  hell and its various circles satan is  also described as having three heads all  three are consuming the bodies of  infamous traitors deep in the frozen  center of the inferno dante's chilling  description was enormously influential  in how artists began to envision satan  there are so many illustrated copies of  dante's inferno that have these  disturbing sketches all of a  three-headed devil  [Music]  due to their growing association with  satan and monsters these three-headed  jesus's became controversial to use  soon religious thinkers called out  against them one of the earliest  examples was sin antoninus of florence  who said that they were quote monstrous  in the nature of things and despite  their original good intention to  illustrate the holy trinity eventually  they were banned entirely in 1628 pope  urban vii outlawed the practice of  depicting god with three heads to  replace them came other more soothing  representations of the trinity often in  paintings that depicted the father as an  elderly man the son is jesus and the  holy spirit as a dove to this day this  motif has become the industry standard  however the practice of depicting jesus  with three heads didn't go away entirely  you see as christianity spread beyond  the borders of europe into the americas  these paintings began to resurface  this painting comes from cusco peru and  was made sometime in the mid 18th  century it's believed that it was used  as a visual teaching aid made by spanish  missionaries to convert the indigenous  inca population you can see it's a  teaching tool he's even holding up the  diagram from before considering that the  image of a three-headed god was  originally adopted by christianity it  seems that we've come full circle now  it's been used by christians to convert  non-christians  from an ancient mythological trope to a  christian symbol to an object of fear  the three-headed jesus has a long  multi-faceted journey to have got here i  certainly think it's one of the most  peculiar images to have come out of this  religion in some ways it's a useful  visual tool to explain the concept of  the trinity the paradoxical idea that  one god exists as three whilst these  images may seem strange to us now it was  once regarded as a sacred icon nowadays  things that don't conform to our visual  standards are often rejected liminal  beings that break the rules of our  natural world  yet there was a time when things that  couldn't be explained through logic were  seen as divine and miraculous that's why  to a secular person like me early art  like this is still so fascinating it  goes against modern aesthetic standards  finding beauty in the bazaar and we've  only just scratched the surface it's  just one of many examples of strange  christian  [Music]  art hey thanks for watching i had a  great time making this video and i'm  looking forward to making more like this  if you like this video and want to see  more why not subscribe a like and a  comment also go a really long way  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time goodbye

